(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_electrochemistry=[{id:"neet-2026r-q049",pn:49,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Electrode potential from Gibbs energy",q:`The standard electrode potential (E\xB0) for the half-cell reaction Fe\xB3\u207A + e\u207B \u2192 Fe\xB2\u207A at 298 K is
(Given : E\xB0(Fe\xB3\u207A/Fe) = \u20130.04 V and E\xB0(Fe\xB2\u207A/Fe) = \u20130.44 V at 298 K)`,o:["+0.76 V","\u20130.48 V","+0.92 V","+0.40 V"],c:0,s:`Fe\xB3\u207A \u2192 Fe (3 electrons, \u20130.04 V) is the sum of Fe\xB3\u207A \u2192 Fe\xB2\u207A (1 electron, E\xB0 unknown) and Fe\xB2\u207A \u2192 Fe (2 electrons, \u20130.44 V).
So \u0394G\xB0(Fe\xB3\u207A/Fe) = \u0394G\xB0(Fe\xB3\u207A/Fe\xB2\u207A) + \u0394G\xB0(Fe\xB2\u207A/Fe).
Use \u0394G\xB0 = \u2013nFE\xB0: \u20133F(\u20130.04) = \u20131F(E\xB0) + (\u20132F)(\u20130.44).
0.12 = \u2013E\xB0 + 0.88.
E\xB0 = 0.88 \u2013 0.12 = 0.76 V.`,sfig:{w:240,h:116,alt:"Cycle: Fe\xB3\u207A to Fe\xB2\u207A with E\xB0 marked ?, Fe\xB2\u207A to Fe marked \u20130.44 V, and Fe\xB3\u207A directly to Fe marked \u20130.04 V.",d:[["t",30,66,"Fe\xB3\u207A"],["l",64,62,104,62,"a"],["t",110,66,"Fe\xB2\u207A"],["l",144,62,184,62,"a"],["t",190,66,"Fe"],["p","M42 52 V28 H120 V50","a"],["t",81,22,"?","m"],["p","M134 52 V28 H198 V50","a"],["t",166,22,"\u20130.44 V","m"],["p","M42 70 V92 H198 V72","a"],["t",120,108,"\u20130.04 V","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q051",pn:51,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Kohlrausch law limiting conductivity",q:`For a salt XY, which is a strong electrolyte, the plot of \u039Bm versus \u221Ac has a slope of \u201390.0 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB3\u141F\xB2 L\xB9\u141F\xB2 at 298 K. At 0.01 M concentration of XY, the value of \u039Bm is 145.0 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9. The limiting molar conductivity of Y\u207B ion (\u03BB\xB0Y\u207B, in S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9) at 298 K will be
(Given : \u03BB\xB0X\u207A = 74.0 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9)`,o:["100.0","90.0","76.0","80.0"],c:3,s:`Debye-Huckel-Onsager: \u039Bm = \u039B\xB0m \u2013 A\u221Ac, a straight line with slope \u2013A = \u201390.
\u221A0.01 = 0.1, so 145 = \u039B\xB0m \u2013 90 \xD7 0.1.
\u039B\xB0m = 154 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9.
\u039B\xB0m(XY) = \u03BB\xB0X\u207A + \u03BB\xB0Y\u207B, so 154 = 74 + \u03BB\xB0Y\u207B.
\u03BB\xB0Y\u207B = 80 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9.`},{id:"neet-2026-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Nernst equation",q:`Calculate emf of the half cell given below:
Pt(s) | H\u2082(g, 2 atm) | HCl(aq, 0.02 M)
E\xB0(H\u2082/H\u207A) = 0
(Given: 2.303RT/F = 0.059, log 2 = 0.3010)`,o:["0.109 V","0.035 V","\u20130.035 V","\u20130.109 V"],c:0,s:`The half-cell reaction is H\u2082(g) \u2192 2H\u207A(aq) + 2e\u207B.
E = E\xB0 \u2013 (2.303RT/nF) log([H\u207A]\xB2/P_H\u2082).
[H\u207A] = 0.02 M (from HCl), P_H\u2082 = 2 atm, 2.303RT/F = 0.059, n = 2.
E = 0 \u2013 (0.059/2) log((0.02)\xB2/2).
= \u20130.0295 log(0.0004/2) = \u20130.0295 log(2\xD710\u207B\u2074).
= \u20130.0295 (log2 + log10\u207B\u2074) = \u20130.0295 (0.301 \u2013 4).
= +0.109 V.`},{id:"neet-2026-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Faraday's laws",q:`A solution of copper sulphate is electrolysed for 10 minutes with a current of 1.5 amperes. The mass of copper deposited at cathode is :
(Given : Molar mass of Cu = 63 g mol\u207B\xB9; 1 F = 96487 C mol\u207B\xB9)`,o:["2.4036 g","1.7018 g","0.5876 g","0.2938 g"],c:3,s:`CuSO\u2084 \u2192 Cu\xB2\u207A + SO\u2084\xB2\u207B.
At the cathode: Cu\xB2\u207A + 2e\u207B \u2192 Cu(s), so equivalent mass E = 63/2.
W = Z i t = (E \xD7 i \xD7 t)/(96500).
W = (63 \xD7 1.5 \xD7 10 \xD7 60)/(2 \xD7 96500) = 56,700/193,000 = 0.2938 g.`},{id:"neet-2025-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Molar conductivity and degree of dissociation",q:`If the molar conductivity (\u039Bm) of a 0.050 mol L\u207B\xB9 solution of a monobasic weak acid is 90 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9, its extent (degree) of dissociation will be
[Assume \u039B\xB0\u208A = 349.6 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9 and \u039B\xB0\u208B = 50.4 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9.]`,o:["0.115","0.125","0.225","0.215"],c:2,s:`Degree of dissociation \u03B1 = \u039Bm/\u039B\xB0m.
\u039B\xB0m = \u039B\xB0\u208A + \u039B\xB0\u208B = 349.6 + 50.4 = 400 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9.
\u03B1 = 90/400 = 0.225.`},{id:"neet-2024-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Faraday's laws of electrolysis",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I (Conversion) \u2014 List-II (Number of Faraday required)
A. 1 mol of H\u2082O to O\u2082 \u2014 I. 3F
B. 1 mol of MnO\u2084\u207B to Mn\xB2\u207A \u2014 II. 2F
C. 1.5 mol of Ca from molten CaCl\u2082 \u2014 III. 1F
D. 1 mol of FeO to Fe\u2082O\u2083 \u2014 IV. 5F
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],c:2,s:`A: 4OH\u207B \u2192 2H\u2082O + O\u2082 + 4e\u207B. For 2 mol of H\u2082O, 4F is needed.
So for 1 mol of H\u2082O, 4F/2 = 2F is needed. A matches II.
B: Mn goes from +7 in MnO\u2084\u207B to +2 in Mn\xB2\u207A, a gain of 5 electrons. So 1 mol needs 5F. B matches IV.
C: Ca\xB2\u207A + 2e\u207B \u2192 Ca, so 1 mol of Ca needs 2F.
For 1.5 mol: 2 \xD7 1.5 = 3F. C matches I.
D: Fe goes from +2 in FeO to +3 in Fe\u2082O\u2083, a loss of 1 electron per Fe. So 1 mol of FeO needs 1F. D matches III.`},{id:"neet-2024-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Faraday's law of electrolysis",q:`Mass in grams of copper deposited by passing 9.6487 A current through a voltmeter containing copper sulphate solution for 100 seconds is :
(Given : Molar mass of Cu : 63 g mol\u207B\xB9, 1F = 96487 C)`,o:["31.5 g","0.0315 g","3.15 g","0.315 g"],c:3,s:`Cu\xB2\u207A(aq) + 2e\u207B \u2192 Cu(s), so n = 2.
Mass deposited w = (M \xD7 i \xD7 t)/(nF).
w = (63 \xD7 9.6487 \xD7 100)/(2 \xD7 96487).
w = 0.315 g.`},{id:"neet-2023-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Cell constant",q:"The conductivity of centimolar solution of KCl at 25\xBAC is 0.0210 ohm\u207B\xB9 and the resistance of the cell containing the solution at 25\xBAC is 60 ohm. The value of cell constant is-",o:["3.34 cm\u207B\xB9","1.34 cm\u207B\xB9","3.28 cm\u207B\xB9","1.26 cm\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`Conductivity \u03BA = conductance G \xD7 cell constant G*.
Conductance G = 1/R, so G* = \u03BA \xD7 R.
G* = 0.0210 \xD7 60 = 1.26 cm\u207B\xB9.`},{id:"neet-2023-q081",pn:81,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Gibbs energy and cell potential",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : In equation \u0394rG = \u2013nFEcell, value of \u0394rG depends on n.
Reason R : Ecell is an intensive property and \u0394rG is an extensive property.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],c:2,s:`In \u0394rG = \u2013nFEcell, n is the number of electrons, so nF is the charge passed. \u0394rG depends on n, so A is true.
Ecell is intensive and \u0394rG is extensive, so R is true.
But R does not explain why \u0394rG depends on n.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q054",pn:54,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"EMF of a cell",q:`Two half cell reactions are given below.
Co\xB3\u207A + e\u207B \u2192 Co\xB2\u207A , E\xB0(Co\xB2\u207A/Co\xB3\u207A) = \u20131.81 V
2Al\xB3\u207A + 6e\u207B \u2192 2Al(s) , E\xB0(Al/Al\xB3\u207A) = +1.66 V
The standard EMF of a cell with feasible redox reaction will be :`,o:["\u20133.47 V","+ 7.09V","+ 0.15V","+ 3.47V"],c:3,s:`Use reduction potentials: E\xB0cell = E\xB0(cathode) \u2013 E\xB0(anode).
The given Co value is for oxidation, so the reduction potential of Co\xB3\u207A/Co\xB2\u207A is +1.81 V; Al\xB3\u207A/Al has \u20131.66 V.
Co\xB3\u207A is reduced (cathode) and Al is oxidised (anode).
E\xB0cell = 1.81 \u2013 (\u20131.66) = 1.81 + 1.66 = 3.47 V.`},{id:"neet-2022-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Spontaneity from E\xB0cell",q:`Given below are half cell reactions:
MnO\u2084\u207B + 8H\u207A + 5e\u207B \u2192 Mn\xB2\u207A + 4H\u2082O, E\xB0(MnO\u2084\u207B/Mn\xB2\u207A) = \u2212E\xB0(Mn\xB2\u207A/MnO\u2084\u207B) = \u22121.510 V
\xBDO\u2082 + 2H\u207A + 2e\u207B \u2192 H\u2082O, E\xB0(O\u2082/H\u2082O) = +1.223 V
Will the permanganate ion, MnO\u2084\u207B liberate O\u2082 from water in the presence of an acid?`,o:["No, because E\xB0cell = \u22120.287 V","Yes, because E\xB0cell = +2.733 V","No, because E\xB0cell = \u22122.733 V","Yes, because E\xB0cell = +0.287 V"],c:3,s:`Take MnO\u2084\u207B reduction (cathode) and water oxidation to O\u2082 (anode).
Multiply the first half reaction by 2 and the water oxidation H\u2082O \u2192 \xBDO\u2082 + 2H\u207A + 2e\u207B by 5.
Net: 2MnO\u2084\u207B + 6H\u207A \u2192 2Mn\xB2\u207A + (5/2)O\u2082 + 3H\u2082O.
E\xB0cell = E\xB0cathode \u2212 E\xB0anode = 1.51 \u2212 1.223 = 0.287 V.
E\xB0cell is positive, so the reaction is spontaneous and MnO\u2084\u207B liberates O\u2082 from water in acid.`},{id:"neet-2022-q085",pn:85,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Electrochemical series and spontaneity",q:`At 298 K, the standard electrode potentials of Cu\xB2\u207A/Cu, Zn\xB2\u207A/Zn, Fe\xB2\u207A/Fe and Ag\u207A/Ag are 0.34 V, \u20130.76 V, \u20130.44 V and 0.80 V, respectively.
On the basis of standard electrode potential, predict which of the following reaction cannot occur?`,o:["CuSO\u2084(aq) + Fe(s) \u2192 FeSO\u2084(aq) + Cu(s)","FeSO\u2084(aq) + Zn(s) \u2192 ZnSO\u2084(aq) + Fe(s)","2CuSO\u2084(aq) + 2Ag(s) \u2192 2Cu(s) + Ag\u2082SO\u2084(aq)","CuSO\u2084(aq) + Zn(s) \u2192 ZnSO\u2084(aq) + Cu(s)"],c:2,s:`A reaction is spontaneous only if E\xB0cell is positive, where E\xB0cell = E\xB0cathode \u2013 E\xB0anode.
FeSO\u2084 + Zn: \u20130.44 \u2013 (\u20130.76) = +0.32 V, possible.
2CuSO\u2084 + 2Ag: 0.34 \u2013 0.80 = \u20130.46 V, not possible.
CuSO\u2084 + Zn: 0.34 \u2013 (\u20130.76) = +1.10 V, possible.
CuSO\u2084 + Fe: 0.34 \u2013 (\u20130.44) = +0.78 V, possible.
So reaction (3) cannot occur.
Note: The printed solution uses 0.80 V for the Cu/Fe cell (1.24 V); the correct value is 0.78 V. Answer unchanged.`},{id:"neet-2022-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Nernst equation",q:`Find the emf of the cell in which the following reaction takes place at 298 K
Ni(s) + 2Ag\u207A (0.001 M) \u2192 Ni\xB2\u207A(0.001 M) + 2Ag(s)
(Given that E\xB0cell = 10.5 V, 2.303RT/F = 0.059 at 298 K)`,o:["1.385 V","0.9615 V","1.05 V","1.0385 V"],a:"NA",s:`Nernst equation: Ecell = E\xB0cell \u2013 (0.059/n) log([Ni\xB2\u207A]/[Ag\u207A]\xB2), with n = 2.
The ratio is (10\u207B\xB3)/(10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 10\xB3, so log = 3.
The correction is 0.0295 \xD7 3 = 0.0885 V.
With the value printed (10.5 V) Ecell = 10.5 \u2013 0.0885 = 10.4115 V, which matches no option.
Note: E\xB0cell is probably 1.05 V; then Ecell = 0.9615 V, option (2).
Note: Printed key is NA (question dropped/ambiguous because E\xB0cell is printed as 10.5 V, a typo for 1.05 V). With 1.05 V: Ecell = 1.05 \u2013 (0.059/2) log(10\u207B\xB3/(10\u207B\xB3)\xB2) = 1.05 \u2013 0.0295 \xD7 3 = 0.9615 V, option 2. With 10.5 V as printed: 10.4115 V, which is not among the options.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q094",pn:94,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Gibbs energy and cell potential",q:`Standard electrode potential for cell with cell reaction
Zn(s) + Cu\xB2\u207A(aq) \u2192 Zn\xB2\u207A(aq) + Cu(s)
is 1.1 V. Calculate the standard Gibbs energy change for the cell reaction.
(Given F = 96487 C mol\u207B\xB9)`,o:["\u2013200.27 J mol\u207B\xB9","\u2013200.27 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","\u2013212.27 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","\u2013212.27 J mol\u207B\xB9"],c:2,s:`\u0394G\xB0 = -nFE\xB0cell.
Here n = 2 and E\xB0cell = 1.1 V.
\u0394G\xB0 = -2 \xD7 96487 \xD7 1.1 = -212271 J mol\u207B\xB9.
This is about -212.27 kJ mol\u207B\xB9.`}]})();
